WEBVTT

00:05.171 --> 00:06.639
My name is Flavin Obiero.

00:06.639 --> 00:10.377
I am currently in Fareham,

00:10.477 --> 00:16.149
Hampshire, south of England,
and I'm a farmer, a butcher and a cook.

00:16.249 --> 00:20.420
We tenanted farm, We've got pigs,
we've got sheep, goats.

00:20.487 --> 00:23.423
We've also got a few chickens for eggs.

00:23.423 --> 00:26.426
We sell all our bodies
directly to the consumer.

00:26.626 --> 00:28.161
So we're coming up to our third year.

00:28.161 --> 00:31.164
So let me go four years
to make this something.

00:31.164 --> 00:33.166
So that pressure is always looming.

00:33.166 --> 00:34.734
And I knew that from the beginning.

00:34.734 --> 00:39.339
We moved to England when I was 15 to join
my dad, who was working here.

00:39.439 --> 00:41.975
I wanted to work with animals,
will be a vet,

00:41.975 --> 00:46.379
and it became clear quite quickly
how much work I needed to do to be a vet.

00:46.446 --> 00:49.449
And I didn't do that work sight.

00:49.649 --> 00:53.520
But after doing a week's work experience
on a farm to gain

00:53.520 --> 00:57.924
practical experience,
I decided to go down that road.

00:57.957 --> 01:00.226
I've always liked the practical side.

01:00.226 --> 01:04.531
It's about me and my 16 year
working in the industry.

01:04.597 --> 01:05.765
I enjoy it.

01:05.765 --> 01:08.668
The highs are very high,
the lows are very low,

01:08.668 --> 01:14.541
and you just got to try and find
a nice medium and cruise on that.

01:14.641 --> 01:18.945
When I first went on a farm
in this country, I was 19.

01:19.045 --> 01:20.146
In Kenya, it's

01:20.146 --> 01:23.983
not a big being in farming
because farming is everywhere.

01:24.017 --> 01:29.556
Like I'd say,
pretty much at least 80, 90% of Kenyans

01:29.656 --> 01:35.295
have a direct link
to a form of food production, whereas here

01:35.395 --> 01:39.566
it used to be like that, but now
it's still be three or four times removed.

01:39.632 --> 01:43.636
So like in agriculture in the UK,

01:43.736 --> 01:47.907
you'll probably count
the number of black people

01:47.974 --> 01:50.009
in the industry actively

01:50.009 --> 01:52.745
on two hands at best,

01:52.745 --> 01:55.482
maybe one foot as well.

01:55.482 --> 01:59.385
So going into it,
I think that was I was naive,

01:59.486 --> 02:01.554
which is probably helped me

02:01.554 --> 02:04.858
because I was quite numb
to a lot of the things that were happening

02:04.858 --> 02:08.962
or things I was hearing,
but it was like going to a new club.

02:09.028 --> 02:13.099
We want everyone to like you and to fit
in, but we would.

02:13.099 --> 02:16.202
Madrid on skin color, obviously
always first people often I say, I'm

02:16.202 --> 02:20.907
a farmer and I love that
because I don't like being put in a box.

02:20.974 --> 02:22.509
I think

02:22.575 --> 02:24.210
as as black

02:24.210 --> 02:28.515
people, we might feel
that it's the in the west

02:28.615 --> 02:32.619
that the countryside is not for us, but

02:32.719 --> 02:34.587
you said that

02:34.587 --> 02:36.556
because if you moved here

02:36.556 --> 02:40.960
from another country, Caribbean or Africa,

02:41.060 --> 02:44.030
you had access to the countryside
wherever you wanted.

02:44.030 --> 02:45.999
So one can have that here.

02:45.999 --> 02:50.236
But because society
thinks moving into cities

02:50.336 --> 02:53.306
is where where the money is, most people

02:53.306 --> 02:57.410
from developing countries are coming to

02:57.510 --> 02:58.411
the west.

02:58.411 --> 03:01.414
You're probably coming from a position of

03:01.447 --> 03:05.285
countryside, living poor,
and you're coming here

03:05.318 --> 03:10.323
with the narrative
that living in the city is progress.

03:10.423 --> 03:16.062
I'm a trustee for a charity that like week
long stay visits on our farms

03:16.162 --> 03:19.032
and then all the kids come from
inner city schools.

03:19.032 --> 03:22.769
And you see some of the kids
when they turn up

03:22.769 --> 03:24.704
at the start of the week,
I don't know when they leave.

03:24.704 --> 03:28.775
It's just like being outside,
being in nature, being around animals,

03:28.775 --> 03:30.510
exchanges, people.

03:30.510 --> 03:34.214
We've all got a right
to exist on this planet.

03:34.314 --> 03:35.548
I don't think that

03:35.548 --> 03:38.351
the lack of people that look like
you should stop

03:38.351 --> 03:43.523
you from going out and living in an area
where

03:43.590 --> 03:44.457
you might enjoy it.

03:44.457 --> 03:48.661
And I think even if you're the first
person that some of these people see,

03:48.728 --> 03:50.830
you're going to make it easier
for the next person.

03:50.830 --> 03:52.565
He's like a chain reaction.

03:52.565 --> 03:57.136
So What I've done with
the little resources we've had so far.

03:57.237 --> 04:02.108
Imagine what we can do with more resources
and more of a name and industry.

04:02.175 --> 04:05.478
So yeah, I've still got still got
plenty in the tank, I'd say.
